Only when there is exactly one parameter and it has no default, no destructuring and no rest. With zero parameters, or two parameters, or any decoration, the parentheses are required.

code:
const one = (n) => n + 1;
const alsoOne = n => n + 1;
const none = () => 'nothing in, one value out';
const two = (a, b) => a + b;
console.log(one(1), alsoOne(1));
console.log(none());
console.log(two(1, 2));

