A tiny helper written as const slug = (s) => s.toLowerCase() still needs its parens once someone adds a second parameter later.

It is this. A normal function gets its own this, set by whoever calls it, while an arrow has none and borrows the this of the code around it. Everything else you have heard, arguments, new, prototype, hoisting, comes back to that one fact.

code:
const counter = {
  count: 7,
  readNormal() {
    return this.count;
  },
  readArrow: () => {
    return this.count;
  },
};
function applyTo(fn) {
  return fn.call(counter);
}
console.log(applyTo(counter.readNormal));
console.log(typeof applyTo(counter.readArrow));

