A page object method that reads this.page has to be a normal function or an arrow with no this, otherwise the test runner gets undefined instead of the page.

Because this is not a normal variable, it is filled in by the engine at call time. An arrow function has no call site to read this from, so it looks one scope outwards and borrows whatever it finds there.

code:
function normal() {
  return this && this.tag;
}
const arrow = () => (this && this.tag) || 'borrowed from the file';
const obj = { tag: 'widget', normal, arrow };
console.log(obj.normal());
console.log(obj.arrow());
console.log(obj.normal() === obj.arrow());

