A wrapper that logs every call needs the real argument list, so it uses (...args) instead of arguments, which arrow functions do not have.

new needs to give the function a this to build an object with. An arrow function refuses to be given a this, so there is nothing to construct and the call is a TypeError.

code:
const makeWidget = (id) => ({ id });
try {
  new makeWidget(1);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.constructor.name + ': ' + err.message);
}
console.log(makeWidget(1));

