Code that does Object.getPrototypeOf(fn).constructor to rebuild a function breaks when fn is an arrow, which is why class methods are preferred over arrows for that.

A generator needs to pause in the middle of its own body, which means it needs its own execution frame and its own this and arguments. An arrow has none of those, so yield is a SyntaxError.

code:
function* counter() {
  yield 1;
  yield 2;
}
const notAGenerator = () => [1, 2, 3];
console.log([...counter()]);
console.log(notAGenerator());
console.log(typeof counter().next);

real example:
A pagination helper that walks a cursor with a generator must be a function declaration, an arrow version fails to parse the whole file.

Whenever you need your own this or your own arguments: object methods, prototype methods, class methods, event handler callbacks that need the element, and anything you want to call with new.

code:
function Timer() {
  this.seconds = 0;
}
Timer.prototype.tick = function () {
  this.seconds += 1;
  return this.seconds;
};
const t = new Timer();
console.log(t.tick(), t.seconds);
const broken = function () {
  return this.seconds;
};
console.log(typeof broken());

