Yes. An arrow function has no name of its own, but if you store it in a const or a property, the engine copies that name onto the function, which is what you see in stack traces.

code:
const named = () => 'stored in a const';
const holder = { onClick: () => 'stored as a property' };
console.log(named.name);
console.log(holder.onClick.name);
console.log((() => {}).name);

