An arrow function is a value assigned to a variable, and a const assignment is not hoisted as a usable function. Calling it above its own line throws a ReferenceError from the temporal dead zone.

code:
try {
  early();
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.constructor.name);
}
function early() {
  return 'declaration';
}
const late = () => 'arrow';
console.log(early(), late());

