You can point the variable at a different function at any time, exactly like any other const variable. You cannot change the arrow function itself, and you cannot add new behaviour to it.

code:
let current = () => 'version one';
console.log(current());
current = () => 'version two';
console.log(current());
const holder = { run: () => 'stored in an object' };
holder.run = () => 'replaced in the object';
