Those methods call your function with a this of undefined, which is what arrow functions assume anyway, so an arrow is the shortest option there. The only difference is that the arrow cannot use arguments, so use the parameters you are given.

code:
const prices = [10, 20, 30, 5];
const doubled = prices.map((p) => p * 2);
const cheap = prices.filter((p) => p < 20);
const total = prices.reduce((acc, p) => acc + p, 0);
console.log(doubled);
console.log(cheap);
console.log(total);

