Building the expected price list for an API assertion with map and an arrow keeps the test readable in one line per case.

map must return one value per item, filter must return true or false, and reduce must return the accumulator every single time. Forget the return in map or reduce and you silently get undefined.

code:
const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4];
console.log(nums.map((n) => {
  n * 2;
}));
console.log(nums.filter((n) => n > 2));
console.log(nums.reduce((acc, n) => {
  acc.push(n);
  return acc;
}, []));

