Writing an arrow function creates a function value, it does not run it. The parentheses after the body are what actually call it, which is why the two forms look so similar but behave differently.

code:
const notRunYet = () => 'I am a value';
const runNow = (() => 'I already ran')();
console.log(typeof notRunYet, notRunYet());
console.log(runNow);
const doubled = ((n) => {
  const inner = () => n * 2;
  return inner();
})(4);
console.log(doubled);

