In a page object you often see both styles in the same file, arrow for simple helpers and normal functions for anything that reads this.

The arrow is created inside the constructor, so the this it borrows is the instance being built, and that reference is kept forever. It is handy for callbacks, but it makes every instance hold its own copy of the function.

code:
class Suite {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
    this.run = async () => 'running ' + this.name;
  }
}
const a = new Suite('checkout');
const b = new Suite('login');
const tasks = [a.run, b.run];
console.log(a.run === b.run);
Promise.all(tasks.map((t) => t())).then(console.log);

