Because the arrow captures the instance at construction time, so subclass overrides and prototype patches never apply, and spying on the method is harder. The method call happens once and is then locked in.

code:
class Base {
  label() {
    return 'base';
  }
}
class Child extends Base {
  label = () => 'frozen at construction';
}
const child = new Child();
Base.prototype.label = () => 'patched later';
console.log(child.label());
console.log(new Base().label());
console.log(child.label === child.label);

