Store it in a const with a meaningful name, or as a named property of an object. The engine reads the name off that storage, so you never have to declare it twice.

code:
const parseHeaders = (raw) => raw.split(',');
const helpers = { parseHeaders };
try {
  parseHeaders();
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.constructor.name);
}
console.log(parseHeaders.name);
console.log(helpers.parseHeaders.name);

