A stack trace from a failing API helper is far easier to follow when the arrow is stored as parseHeaders rather than passed in anonymously.

In modern engines there is none that you can measure in a test. Arrow functions allocate a little differently, but the old advice that they are slower only applied to engines from before 2015.

code:
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}
const addArrow = (a, b) => a + b;
const pairs = Array.from({ length: 100000 }, (_, i) => i);
let t = Date.now();
const a = pairs.map((n) => add(n, 1)).reduce((x, y) => x + y, 0);
const t1 = Date.now() - t;
t = Date.now();
const b = pairs.map((n) => addArrow(n, 1)).reduce((x, y) => x + y, 0);
console.log(a === b);
console.log(typeof t1, typeof (Date.now() - t));

