Optimising a test suite by swapping arrows for function keyword because of speed myths is wasted effort, since the time goes into the browser not the callback.

Every ES module runs in strict mode, and in strict mode a function called with no receiver gets this as undefined. A normal function lets you supply a receiver with call or bind, an arrow has none to supply, so it stays undefined.

code:
'use strict';
const arrow = () => typeof this;
function normal() {
  return typeof this;
}
console.log(normal());
console.log(arrow());
console.log(normal.call({}));

