No. extends needs something it can call with new to build the parent part of the instance, and an arrow refuses that. You get a TypeError at class definition time saying the value is not a constructor.

code:
class Base {
  constructor() {
    this.ok = true;
  }
}
try {
  class Bad extends (() => Base) {}
  console.log(new Bad());
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.constructor.name);
}
console.log(new Base().ok);

