real example:
A helper called with a third retry-count argument keeps working after someone rewrites it as an arrow, it just quietly stops seeing the count.

The method's this is the instance, and the arrow is created while the method runs, so the arrow borrows the instance rather than the file. The same trick in a plain function needs a saved self reference.

code:
class Cart {
  items = ['apple'];
  withArrow() {
    const count = () => this.items.length;
    return count();
  }
  withSelf() {
    const self = this;
    return function () {
      return self.items.length;
    }();
  }
}
const c = new Cart();
console.log(c.withArrow(), c.withSelf());
console.log(c.withArrow === c.withSelf);

